WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
michelle o'neill  you would never accept a trade border  between northern ireland and the  republic so why should unionists accept  one in the irish sea between northern  ireland and the rest of the uk  well dude i don't want to see any  borders we've said from day one that  brexit wasn't compatible with our peace  agreement with our good friday agreement  i've never wanted to see the  interruptions that we're now  experiencing today i think many of us  here warned against the dangers of  brexit and what it actually would mean  for us in reality and we're facing those  challenges today but we sought  along with the majority of other  political parties sought to find  mitigation against the worst impact of  brexit that comes in the form of the  protocol so it protects our peace  agreement it protects the good fruity  agreement it prevents a hard border on  the island of ireland and it ensures the  continuity of trade across this island  which is obviously flourishing as a  direct result of the protocol even if we  accept that it's there as a result of  brexit  you say it protects the peace process  but it has recently led to the  resignation of the first minister so if  it is destabilizing politics here isn't  it time for you to urge the eu to find  something that wins cross-community  support  well it was never cross-community  support for brexit the majority of  people here the majority of political  parties own across community bases voted  to reject brexit yet in all it's foisted  upon us this tory government boris  johnson has shown at every turn that  they do not care less for the people who  live here it is in fact the british  government that negotiated the protocol  it is in fact then the signed up to an  international treaty which they have  been putting in jeopardy ever since the  the ink was barely dry on the agreement  itself so we are not in an ideal  situation however the protocol does give  us the best protection and does afford  us a lot of opportunity what can you say  to reassure unionists that the protocol  is the best solution  can you reassure them that it is in your  view just a trade border and that you  respect the position of northern ireland  constitutionally as part of the united  kingdom i signed up to the good friday  agreement so it'll be the people here  alone that will decide if there's  constitutional change there'll never be  anything wasted upon people i think  unfortunately for the unionist people  for that wider civic society the  unionist leaders have actually completed  the issue of the protocol and brexit a  mess which they single-handedly  delivered alongside their friends in the  tories and but they have completed that  issue of identity with the issue of  trade which comes about as direct result  of brexit so there are two distinctly  different things there's going to be an  election the polls suggest michelle  o'neill  may be northern ireland's next first  minister  given that the ira campaign is still in  people's memories here it's not that  long ago is northern ireland ready for a  sinn fein first minister  i think it's even crazy that you're even  asking me that question because i think  it'll be the people that will decide who  will there who they will elect who they  will they will decide who will be the  largest party like i come from a  background a republican you know i'm a  proud republican i'm somebody who i  believe can govern for all people not  just those republican tradition but  those from other traditions so to sit  here in 2022 for anybody to question a  nationalist ability to be first minister  to hold a position in the legal  profession you know to hold the position  of justice ministry is just absolutely  madness those days are gone but but it's  not just any nationalist politician it  is for many people a party that is  still considered to be the political  wing of the ira no matter how long we  are past ceasefires and good friday  agreement so what do you say to victims  of ira violence for example who would  still have a difficulty with the sinfin  first minister i think that we're a  society that comes from conflict we've  had a very difficult past we have to  recognize that we've had a very  difficult past we also have to recognize  that we've all had a very different  experience of the past my lived  experience would be very different  perhaps to someone else of my uh my  generation some of my peers but we have  to recognize that but we also have to  deal with the past so this british  government that are intent on trying to  cover over what they did here isn't a  good situation because how do we build  for a better future if we don't properly  deal with the past you talk about the  british trying to cover up the past  you're obviously talking about the  proposed amnesty and historical  prosecution of british army veterans  what's the difference though between  those veterans and and former  paramilitaries be they republican or  loyalist who walked free after the good  friday agreement or who were granted in  the case of many in the ira pardons  well firstly again i mean i do not  support in any shape or form an honesty  of any form for anybody so let me be  very clear about that i am 45 years of  age i'm somebody who has been about the  peace process from this inception i'm  someone who believes in the good freddie  agreement i'm somebody who believes in  dealing with the past so that we all can  move forward together the british  government have tried to block every  step forward in dealing with the past  because for those who say draw a line  under it forget that it happened that  doesn't work the impact of conflict is  inter-generational so today's generation  are still fighting the battles of the  past i don't want my ship that for my  children i don't want it for my  grandchildren i don't want it for  anybody else's children if unionists  attempt to block uh sinn fein first  minister by refusing to nominate a  deputy is it time to consider voluntary  coalition so that one of the smaller  parties could nominate a deputy first  minister  well firstly let me say this the days of  democracy and the terms of unionism  alone are gone and they're never going  to return so it'll be the people along  in this election that will decide who  they wish to govern for them and when it  comes to whether or not there should be  a different form of government you have  to remember that we are society coming  out of conflict you have to remember  that we have special and unique  circumstances here so we can't be  looking towards different systems right  now the good friday agreement was an  agreement that a lot of people put a lot  of hard work into nationalist and  unionists republican and loyalists a lot  of sweat went in to bring about a peace  agreement that's something that i hold  very dearly and something that i will  protect at all turns so i believe in the  system of governance as a necessary  piece of governance because of where  we've come from  there's a lot of speculation that you  will be first minister of northern  ireland and that your party leader mary  lou mcdonald will be the next taoiseach  the next irish prime minister  if that scenario arises  do you think that immediately opens the  door to your referendum on irish unity  well i certainly think that we're closer  than we ever have been previously and i  think now's the time to plan and that's  why we're saying to the irish government  now is the time for them to make the  preparation let's not fall into the  traps that have been fallen to in terms  of brexit make the preparation let's  start to have a healthy conversation  about the things that matter every day  to people what does the health service  look like in a united ireland what does  education look like in the united  ireland so i think that this is a  hopeful conversation because our society  here has lived apart for far too long  because if the last couple of years of  brexit has told us anything is that the  tories will never prioritize the  interests of people here whether you be  unionist nationalist or other earlier  this week people were surprised to hear  your party leader send platinum jubilee  greetings to the queen we've come a long  way from the days when jerry adams was  describing her as mrs windsor  was that just electioneering a reaching  out to the unionist community or the  moderate middle ground community or do  you genuinely respect queen elizabeth of  course i mean i think we have to i mean  we live in a society that's been so  divided and i respect the views of those  of unions that entity those with the  british identity and those people that  look towards the monarchy it's not my  thing but that's okay i can be  respectful enough to understand that for  those for other people in my society who  my community that is important to them  so that's why we sent our best wishes  and you know i do so again today on your  program  michelle o'neill thanks very much thank  you

